export const EN_BODIES = {
  'yeast-types-for-baking': `<p>Yeast is one of the most important ingredients in a bread recipe, and it can have a greater effect on the result than many other ingredients. If you have seen fresh, dry, and instant yeast on store shelves and wondered how they differ, this guide is for you.</p><p>We introduce the main types of yeast, compare their differences, and help you choose the right one for your needs—whether you bake at home, run a bakery, or work on an industrial production line.</p><h2>What is yeast, and how does it work?</h2><p>Yeast is a single-celled fungus, usually the species Saccharomyces cerevisiae. Its cells consume the sugars in dough and produce carbon dioxide, some alcohol, and aromatic compounds. The carbon dioxide becomes trapped in the dough’s gluten network, causing the dough to rise and contributing to the bread’s flavour and aroma.</p><p>The main differences among baker’s yeasts are their moisture content, appearance, handling, and shelf life. Their essential job—making dough rise—is the same.</p><h2>Three main types of yeast</h2><p>A useful way to classify the common yeasts on the market is to group them into three types:</p><ol><li>Fresh yeast</li><li>Active dry yeast</li><li>Instant yeast</li></ol><h3>1. Fresh yeast</h3><p>Fresh, or compressed, yeast is sold as a soft block that can be crumbled. It has a high moisture content, around 70%. Because its cells are alive and hydrated, it must be refrigerated and usually lasts only a few weeks. It has a distinctive aroma and is still used in many traditional bakeries and pastry shops.</p><p>Advantages: fast activity and a pleasant aroma in some breads.</p><p>Limitations: it needs a cold chain, has a short shelf life, is harder to transport and store, and can lose quality in warm conditions.</p><figure><img alt="1. Fresh yeast" src="/assets/blog/yeast-types-for-baking/image-01.webp"></figure><h3>2. Active dry yeast</h3><p>In active dry yeast, the cells are dried into relatively large granules, with a moisture content of about 7–8%. Before use, this type should be activated in lukewarm water at around 35–40 °C with a little sugar until it becomes foamy, then added to the dough. It lasts much longer than fresh yeast and does not need refrigeration while unopened.</p><p>Advantages: good shelf life and convenient storage. Limitations: it needs an activation step and is sensitive to water temperature.</p><h3>3. Instant dry yeast</h3><p>Instant dry yeast has finer granules and less moisture, around 4–6%. Its main advantage is that it does not need to be activated: it can be mixed directly with flour, with fast and consistent performance. This has made it widely used in industrial and semi-industrial bakeries, bread and dough-product factories, and home kitchens.</p><p>Advantages: easy to use, steady activity, long shelf life, and suitability for vacuum packaging. Limitation: once opened, the package must be kept away from air and moisture.</p><h2>Fresh, active dry, and instant yeast compared</h2><figure data-rich-table=""><table><caption>Comparison of fresh, active dry, and instant yeast</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Instant</th><th scope="col">Active dry</th><th scope="col">Fresh</th><th scope="col">Feature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>About 4–6%</td><td>About 7–8%</td><td>About 70%</td><td>Approximate moisture</td></tr><tr><td>Fine granules</td><td>Large granules</td><td>Soft block</td><td>Appearance</td></tr><tr><td>Not required</td><td>Required (lukewarm water)</td><td>Usually not required; crumble and dissolve</td><td>Activation</td></tr><tr><td>Cool, dry room temperature</td><td>Cool, dry room temperature</td><td>Refrigerated</td><td>Storage</td></tr><tr><td>Long</td><td>Long</td><td>A few weeks</td><td>Shelf life</td></tr><tr><td>Bakery, industry, and home use</td><td>Home baking and bakeries</td><td>Traditional and short-term use</td><td>Best suited to</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><h2>Converting quantities between yeast types</h2><p>If a recipe calls for one type and you have another, these approximate ratios can help:</p><ul><li>One part instant yeast is roughly equivalent to three parts fresh yeast.</li><li>One part active dry yeast is roughly equivalent to two parts fresh yeast.</li><li>The exact amount depends on the flour, room temperature, and proofing time. It is best to fine-tune the quantity with a small trial batch.</li></ul><img alt="Approximate conversion between yeast types" src="/assets/blog/yeast-types-for-baking/image-02.webp"><h2>Other types of yeast you may hear about</h2><ul><li>Sourdough starter: a culture of yeasts and lactic-acid bacteria grown in dough, giving bread a tangy flavour and distinctive keeping qualities.</li><li>Inactive yeast, often called nutritional yeast: it is not used for fermentation and is mainly used as a seasoning or food supplement.</li><li>Brewer’s yeast: used in brewing and some supplements; it is not a suitable replacement for baker’s yeast.</li></ul><h2>Which yeast is best?</h2><p>The answer to “Which yeast is best?” depends on what you need:</p><ul><li>Home baking: small packs of instant yeast, such as 70–125 g, are a simple, convenient choice.</li><li>Traditional or commercial bakeries: instant or dry yeast with consistent quality is useful because repeatable results matter in every bake.</li><li>Industrial bakeries: accurate dosing, consistency between batches, and larger packs such as 5 or 10 kg are especially important on high-volume lines. Dry yeast is also much easier to store.</li><li>Long-term storage: instant and dry yeast in vacuum packs are usually the best options.</li></ul><h2>Important points when choosing and buying yeast</h2><p>Before comparing yeast prices, check the following:</p><ul><li>The production and expiry dates and the storage instructions on the pack.</li><li>The package type—vacuum pack, sachet, or bag—and whether its size matches your usage.</li><li>The manufacturer’s certificates and standards, such as ISO 9001, ISO 22000, HACCP, and halal certification.</li><li>Consistent quality in future purchases, not just the quality of one sample.</li></ul><p>For more detail on how to evaluate a purchase, read the <a href="/en/blog/yeast-buying-guide">complete yeast buying guide</a>.</p><figure><img alt="Important points when choosing and buying yeast" src="/assets/blog/yeast-types-for-baking/image-03.webp"></figure><h2>Buying yeast from Khuzestan Yeast</h2><p><a href="/en">Khuzestan Yeast Co.</a> has operated in Dezful since 2002 and produces instant yeast using European technology and the experience of Iranian specialists. Its products are sold under four brands—Dezmaye, Nanmaye, Shetab, and X Power—in pack sizes from 70 g to 10 kg, giving home bakers, traditional bakeries, and industrial users options to suit their needs.</p><ul><li>Visit the <a href="/en/products">products page</a> to see the yeast range and request pricing.</li><li>If you need dry yeast for everyday use, choose from the available pack sizes.</li><li>For larger requirements, bakeries and industrial businesses can ask about wholesale yeast and suitable packaging.</li></ul><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><p>What is the difference between instant and active dry yeast?</p><p>Instant yeast has finer granules and can be mixed directly with flour without activation. Active dry yeast is coarser and should be activated in lukewarm water before use.</p><p>Can fresh yeast be replaced with instant yeast?</p><p>Yes. Use an approximate conversion of one part instant yeast to three parts fresh yeast, then adjust the amount with a small trial for a more precise result.</p><p>How should instant yeast be stored?</p><p>Keep unopened packs in a cool, dry place. After opening, seal the pack tightly and store it somewhere cool—refrigerate it if the product instructions recommend this—and use it within the stated period.</p><p>Is baker’s yeast different from yeast for home baking?</p><p>The yeast type itself is not different. The main differences are pack size, usage volume, and consistency between production batches.</p><h2>Summary</h2><p>In brief, fresh yeast has high moisture and a short shelf life; active dry yeast needs activation; and instant yeast is the simplest, most consistent choice for many modern uses. The best option depends on how much you use, your storage conditions, and the result you want from your bread. For product information and purchasing advice, contact the Khuzestan Yeast team.</p>`,

  'fresh-vs-dry-yeast': `<h2>Introduction</h2><p>Every baker, producer, or wholesale buyer eventually asks: is fresh yeast better than dry yeast? The answer depends on how the product will be used, available storage, production volume, and even the destination market. This article compares fresh and dry yeast by moisture, shelf life, use, and application to help you make a better choice. If you are planning a purchase, our <a href="/en/blog/yeast-buying-guide">complete yeast buying guide</a> may also help.</p><h2>What is fresh yeast?</h2><p>Fresh yeast, also called wet or compressed yeast, is sold in soft, moist blocks. Its moisture content is high, and the living yeast cells are kept in a hydrated environment. As a result, it must be refrigerated and has a short shelf life, usually only a few weeks.</p><figure><img alt="What is fresh yeast?" src="/assets/blog/fresh-vs-dry-yeast/image-02.webp"></figure><h2>What is dry yeast?</h2><p>Dry yeast is supplied as granules or small particles after its moisture has been reduced through drying. It contains much less moisture than fresh yeast, making it easier to store and transport. The two common types are:</p><ul><li><strong>Active dry yeast:</strong> usually needs to be activated in lukewarm water before use.</li><li><strong>Instant yeast:</strong> has finer granules and is usually mixed directly with flour. See our <a href="/en/products">instant yeast products</a> for more information.</li></ul><h2>Fresh and dry yeast at a glance</h2><figure data-rich-table=""><table><caption>Fresh and dry yeast at a glance</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Fresh yeast</th><th scope="col">Dry yeast</th><th scope="col">Feature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Soft, moist block</td><td>Granules or small particles</td><td>Appearance</td></tr><tr><td>High</td><td>Very low</td><td>Moisture</td></tr><tr><td>Refrigerated</td><td>Room temperature, according to the manufacturer’s instructions</td><td>Storage</td></tr><tr><td>Short (a few weeks)</td><td>Long (months, up to two years)</td><td>Shelf life</td></tr><tr><td>Crumble and dissolve in water or dough</td><td>Active dry: activate; instant: mix directly with flour</td><td>Use</td></tr><tr><td>Delicate; requires a cold chain</td><td>Easier and better suited to distant markets</td><td>Transport and distribution</td></tr><tr><td>Suitable for rapid use</td><td>Suitable for ongoing use and storage</td><td>Typical use</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><h2>Fresh and dry yeast in practice</h2><h3>1. Shelf life and storage</h3><p>The biggest difference between fresh and dry yeast is shelf life. Fresh yeast must be kept cold and used quickly, while dry yeast can be stored for much longer. For example, Khuzestan Yeast vacuum packs have a shelf life of up to 24 months, while the 10 kg bag has a six-month shelf life, provided the recommended storage conditions are followed.</p><figure><img alt="1. Shelf life and storage" src="/assets/blog/fresh-vs-dry-yeast/image-03.webp"></figure><h3>2. How to use it</h3><p>Fresh yeast is usually crumbled and dissolved in water or in the dough. Instant yeast needs no separate activation and can be mixed directly with flour. Active dry yeast usually needs lukewarm water. Always follow the manufacturer’s recommended quantity.</p><h3>3. Transport, distribution, and logistics costs</h3><p>Fresh yeast needs refrigerated equipment, which makes long-distance transport and export markets more challenging. Because dry yeast has low moisture, it is easier to transport and distribute. This is why many wholesalers and exporters choose dry yeast. Read about <a href="/en/products">wholesale yeast options</a> for more information.</p><h3>4. Performance consistency</h3><p>Fresh yeast can perform well when stored correctly, but its short shelf life requires rapid stock turnover. With dry yeast, vacuum packaging and quality control can help maintain consistency over time. For more advice on evaluating a product, read our guide to <a href="/en/blog/how-to-identify-quality-yeast">identifying good-quality yeast</a>.</p><h2>Fresh-to-dry yeast quantities</h2><p>How much fresh or dry yeast should you use? Dry yeast is more concentrated than fresh yeast, so you generally need less. A common approximate conversion is:</p><figure data-rich-table=""><table><caption>Fresh and dry yeast quantities</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Fresh yeast</th><th scope="col">Instant yeast (approximate)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3 measures</td><td>1 measure</td></tr><tr><td>30 g</td><td>About 10 g</td></tr><tr><td>100 g</td><td>About 33 g</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><p>This ratio is approximate and varies by brand, formula, and dough conditions. For professional production, determine the exact ratio through testing and by following the manufacturer’s advice.</p><figure><img alt="Fresh-to-dry yeast quantities" src="/assets/blog/fresh-vs-dry-yeast/image-04.webp"></figure><h2>Advantages of dry yeast</h2><ul><li>Long shelf life and the ability to keep a reserve.</li><li>Easier transport and distribution, especially to distant markets and for export.</li><li>Less need for refrigerated equipment and a lower risk of spoilage.</li><li>Simple to use, particularly in instant form.</li><li>A range of pack sizes, from home use to industrial production.</li><li>Easier purchasing and inventory planning.</li></ul><p>The Khuzestan Yeast catalogue lists pack formats from 70 g to 10 kg for household, commercial, and industrial needs.</p><h2>Advantages of fresh yeast</h2><p>Fresh yeast also has advantages and is preferred in some bakeries and recipes:</p><ul><li>It is available in some local markets.</li><li>Many bakers have long experience and established routines with it.</li><li>It suits rapid use and high stock turnover.</li></ul><p>These advantages matter when cold storage and rapid use are practical for your operation.</p><figure><img alt="Advantages of fresh yeast" src="/assets/blog/fresh-vs-dry-yeast/image-05.webp"></figure><h2>Which should you choose?</h2><ul><li>A bakery with steady daily use and suitable refrigeration: either type may work, but dry yeast is easier to manage. See our guide to <a href="/en/blog/yeast-types-for-baking">baker’s yeast</a> for more details.</li><li>A factory or industrial production line: dry yeast is often the more practical choice because of its consistency, shelf life, and ease of transport.</li><li>A wholesaler or distributor: dry yeast is a common choice because it stores and ships easily.</li><li>Export: dry yeast is almost always the more practical option.</li></ul><p>When comparing costs, do not look only at the price per kilogram. Storage, waste, transport, and performance consistency all affect the final cost. If you are buying dry yeast, ask the supplier for quality documents and storage instructions.</p><h2>Common mistakes when comparing and choosing</h2><ul><li>Assuming fresh and dry yeast are used in the same quantities.</li><li>Ignoring storage conditions and expiry dates.</li><li>Comparing products only by price per kilogram.</li><li>Using active dry yeast in place of instant yeast without the required activation.</li><li>Keeping an opened pack in a warm, humid place.</li></ul><figure><img alt="Common mistakes when comparing and choosing" src="/assets/blog/fresh-vs-dry-yeast/image-06.webp"></figure><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><p>What is the main difference between fresh and dry yeast?</p><p>Fresh yeast has high moisture, a short shelf life, and must be kept cold. Dry yeast has low moisture, keeps longer, and is easier to transport.</p><p>Can fresh yeast be replaced with dry yeast?</p><p>Yes, if you adjust the amount. As a general guide, use about one-third as much instant yeast as fresh yeast, then test to find the right ratio.</p><p>Is instant yeast different from active dry yeast?</p><p>Yes. Instant yeast is finer and is usually mixed directly with flour; active dry yeast usually needs to be activated in lukewarm water.</p><p>Which type is better for wholesale buying?</p><p>Dry yeast is usually more suitable for wholesale and distribution because it lasts longer, is easier to transport, and comes in varied pack sizes.</p><p>How long does dry yeast last?</p><p>It depends on the brand and packaging. The Khuzestan Yeast catalogue lists up to 24 months for vacuum packs and six months for the 10 kg bag, provided storage conditions are followed.</p><h2>Summary</h2><p>Fresh and dry yeast differ in moisture, shelf life, use, and logistics. Fresh yeast can suit rapid, local use, while dry yeast—with its longer shelf life, easier transport, and varied packaging—is often more practical for bakeries, factories, wholesalers, and exporters. Contact the Khuzestan Yeast team for product information and enquiries.</p>`,

  'instant-vs-active-dry-yeast': `<p>If you have ever stood in front of the baking aisle and wondered, “Instant or active yeast?”, you are not alone. Both are dry yeasts, both ferment dough, and they may even appear side by side on a shelf—but they behave differently in practice. Understanding the difference can help you make more consistent dough, bake better-volume bread, and reduce production waste.</p><p>This guide explains what instant and active yeast are, how they differ, and which one to choose for different situations. If you are planning a purchase, do not miss our <a href="/en/blog/yeast-buying-guide">complete yeast buying guide</a>.</p><h2>What is active dry yeast?</h2><p><strong>Active dry yeast</strong> is made from living Saccharomyces cerevisiae cells. After cultivation, the cells are dewatered and dried into relatively large granules. A layer of dried cells covers the outside of each granule and helps protect the living cells inside during storage.</p><p>For this reason, active dry yeast usually needs to be activated in lukewarm water before use, a step also called proofing or pre-activation. If it is sprinkled directly onto flour, some granules may not dissolve properly, leading to slower and less even fermentation.</p><video preload="metadata" controls><source type="video/mp4" src="/assets/blog/instant-vs-active-dry-yeast/video-1.mp4"></video><h2>What is instant yeast?</h2><p><a href="/en/products"><strong>Instant dry yeast</strong></a> is the same yeast species, but a different drying process produces finer, more porous granules. They absorb water more quickly and do not require a separate activation step. Bakers and producers can mix instant yeast directly with flour and start working.</p><p>This ease of use and consistent result have made instant yeast popular in industrial bakeries, large production facilities, and home kitchens.</p><h2>Instant and active yeast compared</h2><figure data-rich-table=""><table><caption>Differences between instant and active yeast</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Instant yeast</th><th scope="col">Active dry yeast</th><th scope="col">Feature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fine and uniform</td><td>Coarser</td><td>Granule size</td></tr><tr><td>Directly into the flour</td><td>Activate in lukewarm water first</td><td>How to use</td></tr><tr><td>Faster</td><td>Slower (after activation)</td><td>Start of fermentation</td></tr><tr><td>Usually less</td><td>Usually more</td><td>Amount required</td></tr><tr><td>Very consistent</td><td>Depends on proper activation</td><td>Dough consistency</td></tr><tr><td>Production lines, bakeries, everyday use</td><td>Traditional methods and recipes that call for activation</td><td>Best suited to</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><h2>Key practical differences</h2><h3>1. How to use it</h3><p>The main difference between instant and active dry yeast is how they are used. Active dry yeast needs a few minutes in lukewarm water; instant yeast can be used without that step. In a busy bakery, skipping the activation step saves time and reduces operator error.</p><h3>2. Fermentation speed</h3><p>Because its granules are finer, instant yeast disperses through the dough more quickly and starts fermentation sooner. This matters in processes where proofing times are planned precisely.</p><p><img alt="2. Fermentation speed" src="/assets/blog/instant-vs-active-dry-yeast/image-02.webp"></p><h3>3. Dosage</h3><p>In general, a greater share of the cells in instant yeast remains alive and active, so a smaller amount may be needed. A common recipe guideline is to use about <strong>three-quarters</strong> as much instant yeast as active dry yeast. The exact amount should still be set according to the product specification and testing in your own formula.</p><h3>4. Temperature and sensitivity to mistakes</h3><p>If the activation water for active dry yeast is too hot or too cold, some cells may be damaged or fail to activate. Since instant yeast is mixed directly into the dough, it is less vulnerable to this particular mistake. However, dough temperature remains important for fermentation in both cases.</p><h3>5. Shelf life and storage</h3><p>The shelf life of both types depends on production quality, packaging, and storage conditions. Vacuum-packed products usually keep longer. After opening, store the yeast in a sealed container in a cool, dry place away from moisture.</p><p><img alt="5. Shelf life and storage" src="/assets/blog/instant-vs-active-dry-yeast/image-03.webp"></p><h2>Instant or active yeast: which is better?</h2><p>The answer to “Which is better, instant or active yeast?” depends on your needs:</p><ul><li><strong>Traditional and industrial bakeries:</strong> instant yeast is a common choice because it is quick and easy to use.</li><li><strong>Food manufacturers and production lines:</strong> the repeatable results of instant yeast make process control easier.</li><li><strong>Home baking:</strong> instant yeast is convenient; active dry yeast also works when the recipe calls for activation.</li><li><strong>Traditional recipes:</strong> some recipes are written around activating yeast in water and sugar, so you can keep using that method.</li></ul><p>Yeast quality and production consistency matter more than the type alone. A high-quality instant yeast made under careful process control can give a better result than an unreliable product.</p><h2>Instant yeast and fresh yeast</h2><p>Fresh, or compressed, yeast is another option. It has high moisture, needs refrigeration, and has a shorter shelf life. To compare all the main types, read our article on <a href="/en/blog/yeast-types-for-baking">types of yeast</a>. For more information about yeast used in production, visit our <a href="/en/products">baker’s yeast product page</a>.</p><h2>Tips for using yeast correctly</h2><ul><li>Before use, check the production and expiry dates and the condition of the package.</li><li>If you use active dry yeast, use lukewarm water and avoid hot water.</li><li>Do not leave yeast in prolonged direct contact with salt.</li><li>Keep an opened pack sealed, cool, and away from moisture.</li><li>Test every new formula on a small scale first.</li></ul><h2>How to choose good-quality yeast</h2><p>Do not choose yeast by type alone. Also consider these points:</p><ul><li>A reputable manufacturer with a track record and quality- and food-safety management systems.</li><li>Available documentation and certificates, such as ISO and halal certification.</li><li>Pack sizes suited to your use, from small household packs to professional bakery and industrial formats.</li><li>Consistent quality across production batches.</li></ul><p><a href="/en">Khuzestan Yeast Co.</a> has produced instant dry yeast in Dezful since 2002. Its products are available in pack sizes from 70 g to 10 kg under four brands: <a href="/en/products#dezmaye">Dezmaye</a>, <a href="/en/products#nanmaye">Nanmaye</a>, <a href="/en/products#shetab">Shetab</a>, and <a href="/en/products#xpower">X Power</a>. For current <a href="/en/products">yeast prices</a> and dry-yeast purchasing information, <a href="/en/contact">contact the sales team</a>. Bakeries, distributors, and food producers can also ask about <a href="/en/contact">wholesale yeast</a>.</p><p><img alt="How to choose good-quality yeast" src="/assets/blog/instant-vs-active-dry-yeast/image-04.webp"></p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><p><strong>Can instant yeast replace active dry yeast?</strong> In most cases, yes. You may need a smaller amount, and you can usually skip the activation step. Test to determine the exact dose.</p><p><strong>Does instant yeast need to be dissolved in water?</strong> Not necessarily. It is designed to mix directly with flour. Dissolving it in lukewarm water is also possible if you prefer.</p><p><strong>What is the difference between instant and dry yeast?</strong> In everyday usage, “dry yeast” may refer to either type. The main differences are granule size, preparation, and how quickly fermentation starts.</p><p><strong>Which type is better for a bakery?</strong> Instant yeast is practical for most bakeries because it is easy to use and gives consistent results.</p><h2>Summary</h2><p>The main difference between instant and active dry yeast is the granule structure and how each is used: active dry yeast has larger granules and should be activated; instant yeast is finer and can be mixed directly with flour. If speed, convenience, and consistency matter to you, instant yeast is a practical choice. In either case, production quality and proper storage determine the final result.</p>`,

  'how-to-identify-quality-yeast': `<p>Every baker and home cook who has waited hours for dough to rise knows how poor-quality yeast can waste a day’s work. Dough that does not rise, bread that turns hard and dense, or a slightly bitter or sour taste may be caused by the yeast rather than your recipe or skill. Knowing how to identify good yeast is valuable for bakeries and production facilities as well as home users.</p><p>This guide explains the qualities of good yeast, simple ways to test it, and what to check when buying so you can avoid counterfeit or old products.</p><h2>Why does yeast quality matter so much?</h2><p>Yeast is a living organism. Its cells become active in the presence of water, warmth, and sugar and produce carbon dioxide. That gas inflates the dough and creates bread’s airy texture. The more living, active cells there are, the more even and predictable fermentation will be. Weak yeast can cause:</p><ul><li>Slow or incomplete rising and the need to use more yeast.</li><li>Dense, heavy bread.</li><li>Unpleasant flavour or smell.</li><li>Different results from one bake to the next—a direct loss for commercial bakeries.</li><li>More bread waste and higher production costs.</li></ul><p>Investing in good-quality yeast is therefore a way to reduce hidden costs, even if the purchase price is a little higher.</p><figure><img alt="Why yeast quality matters" src="/assets/blog/how-to-identify-quality-yeast/image-02.webp"></figure><h2>What are the signs of good yeast?</h2><p>Before doing any test, you can check several signs of good yeast as soon as you open the pack—or even before opening it.</p><h3>1. Appearance and colour</h3><p>Good-quality instant dry yeast looks like fine, uniform, cylindrical or round granules, with a cream to light-brown colour. A large amount of powder, coarse or irregular granules, unusual darkening, or coloured spots may indicate moisture damage, poor storage, or low quality.</p><h3>2. Smell</h3><p>Healthy yeast has a mild smell like yeast or fresh bread. If an opened pack smells sour, mouldy, strongly alcoholic, or otherwise unpleasant, do not use it.</p><h3>3. Free-flowing granules with no clumps</h3><p>Properly packaged dry yeast should be loose and free-flowing. Clumps or granules stuck together may mean moisture has entered the pack or the seal was not made properly.</p><h3>4. Intact, vacuum-sealed packaging</h3><p>Packaging is one of the most important factors in preserving quality. Vacuum packs should be firm and compressed, with the air removed. A swollen or loose pack, or one with a tear or pinhole, may have let in oxygen and moisture, reducing yeast activity.</p><figure><img alt="4. Intact, vacuum-sealed packaging" src="/assets/blog/how-to-identify-quality-yeast/image-03.webp"></figure><h3>5. Production and expiry dates</h3><p>The production and expiry dates should be clear and readable. Packs with missing information, erased dates, or signs of tampering are suspicious. Shelf life varies by package type: vacuum packs generally last longer than sachets and larger bags, and storage conditions also matter.</p><h2>Testing yeast quality at home or at work</h2><p>Appearance and smell tell only part of the story. For more confidence, do a simple yeast-quality test that takes less than 15 minutes.</p><h3>The activation test (proofing)</h3><ol><li>Prepare a glass of lukewarm water at about 35–40 °C. It should be neither cold nor hot; hot water kills the cells.</li><li>Dissolve one teaspoon of sugar in the water.</li><li>Sprinkle about one tablespoon of yeast over the water without stirring.</li><li>Wait 10–15 minutes.</li></ol><p>Result: if the surface becomes foamy and bubbly, a creamy layer forms, and there is a fermented aroma, the yeast is active and healthy. If nothing happens or there is very little foam, the yeast may be weak, old, or unsuitable.</p><figure><img alt="Yeast activation test (proofing)" src="/assets/blog/how-to-identify-quality-yeast/image-04.webp"></figure><h3>A small-dough test</h3><p>For bakeries and production facilities, the best test is a trial under real working conditions. Make a small batch using a fixed formula and record the proofing time, increase in volume, and bread texture. Using the same recipe and temperature each time lets you compare different batches or brands fairly. Keeping these records over time can show whether your supplier provides consistent quality.</p><h2>Good-quality yeast at a glance</h2><p>In summary, good-quality yeast has these features:</p><ul><li>Uniform, free-flowing granules with no clumps.</li><li>Natural colour and a mild smell.</li><li>Intact, firmly sealed packaging, especially vacuum packaging.</li><li>Clear production date, expiry date, and batch number.</li><li>Fast, even dough rise in an activation test.</li><li>Consistent results between batches.</li><li>Bread with good volume, an even texture, and a mild flavour.</li></ul><h2>How can counterfeit yeast be identified?</h2><p>Similar-looking packages are common, and counterfeit or repackaged yeast appears in some sales channels. Watch for these warning signs:</p><ul><li><strong>Unusually low price:</strong> ask why a price is far below the market average. It may indicate low quality, an expiry date that is near, or a non-genuine product.</li><li><strong>Poor packaging:</strong> blurry printing, dull colours, spelling errors, and untidy labels are common signs of copying.</li><li><strong>Missing manufacturer details:</strong> the factory name, address, phone number, and production information should be available on the pack.</li><li><strong>An unverified seller:</strong> do not buy from a seller who cannot provide the source, invoice, or product permits.</li><li><strong>Opened or repackaged product:</strong> yeast transferred from a large bag into smaller packs without proper vacuum sealing and production information cannot be relied on.</li></ul><figure><img alt="How to identify counterfeit yeast" src="/assets/blog/how-to-identify-quality-yeast/image-05.webp"></figure><h2>How to identify a reputable yeast supplier</h2><p>A reputable yeast product depends on more than good-looking packaging; it should come from a manufacturer with a quality-control system. When choosing a brand or supplier, check for:</p><ul><li>Quality-management and food-safety certificates such as ISO 9001, ISO 22000, and HACCP.</li><li>Halal certification for consumers who require it.</li><li>A certificate of analysis (COA) and a material safety data sheet (MSDS), available on request.</li><li>A proven history of production and supply and a stable presence in the market.</li><li>Clear storage and shelf-life information for each package type.</li></ul><p>For example, <a href="/en">Khuzestan Yeast Co.</a> has produced instant dry yeast in Dezful since 2002. With European production technology and the expertise of Iranian specialists, it offers four brands: Dezmaye, Nanmaye, Shetab, and X Power. The company holds ISO 9001:2015, ISO 22000:2018, HACCP, ISO 14001:2015, ISO 45001:2018, and halal certificates, and can provide further documentation on request.</p><h2>Storage practices that preserve quality</h2><p>Even the best yeast can lose quality if stored incorrectly. Follow a few simple rules:</p><ul><li>Keep unopened packs in a cool, dry place away from direct sunlight.</li><li>After opening, remove excess air, seal the pack tightly, and preferably refrigerate it; use it within a short time.</li><li>Keep yeast away from heat, steam, and moisture, such as near a stove or sink.</li><li>Larger packs, such as 10 kg bags, have a shorter shelf life. Buy according to your usage rate and manage stock by production date using FIFO (first in, first out).</li></ul><h2>Buying guide: what should you check before ordering?</h2><p>Once you understand quality, the next step is choosing where to buy. Small vacuum packs are often a good fit for home users and small businesses; 5 kg and 10 kg packs may be more economical for bakeries and industrial facilities. In every case, evaluate the price together with quality, packaging, production date, and supplier reliability—do not choose only by the lowest number. If you need a regular supply, buying wholesale yeast directly from a manufacturer can often provide steadier pricing and more consistent quality.</p><figure><img alt="Buying guide: what to check before ordering" src="/assets/blog/how-to-identify-quality-yeast/image-06.webp"></figure><p>For a complete overview of selecting and comparing brands, read our <a href="/en/blog/yeast-buying-guide">complete yeast buying guide</a>. If you are looking for baker’s yeast with a reliable shelf life and performance, or want to learn how to identify fresh yeast, our related articles can help. You can also visit the <a href="/en/products">dry yeast products page</a>, or <a href="/en/contact">contact us about current prices and wholesale yeast</a>.</p><h2>A quick checklist for identifying good-quality yeast</h2><ul><li>The pack is intact, firm, and vacuum-sealed.</li><li>The production and expiry dates are readable.</li><li>The granules are uniform, free-flowing, and not clumped.</li><li>The smell is natural, not sour or mouldy.</li><li>It foams within 10–15 minutes in an activation test.</li><li>The manufacturer has quality certificates and documentation.</li><li>The price is reasonable and the seller can be verified.</li></ul><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can expired yeast be used?</h3><p>Yeast activity usually decreases gradually after its expiry date. If it foams in an activation test, it may work when used in a larger amount, but this is not recommended for professional production because the result may be inconsistent.</p><h3>What is the difference between instant dry yeast and fresh yeast?</h3><p>Instant dry yeast has low moisture, a long shelf life, and can be mixed directly with flour. Fresh, or compressed, yeast has high moisture and must be refrigerated and used within a short time.</p><h3>Why is my dough not rising even though I bought fresh yeast?</h3><p>Common causes include water that is too hot or too cold, salt placed directly on the yeast, a low room temperature, too little yeast, or a pack that was not stored properly after opening.</p><h3>How much yeast should I use?</h3><p>The amount depends on the formula and room temperature. Follow the recipe and package directions; in professional production, use small trials to find the optimum amount.</p><h2>Summary</h2><p>Three simple steps help identify good yeast: inspect its appearance, packaging, and dates; run an activation test; and check the source. Good-quality yeast gives you more consistent dough, better bread, and less waste. By watching for warning signs, avoiding suspicious products, and choosing a transparent supplier with supporting documents, you can buy with greater confidence for your bakery or home.</p>`,

  'buy-yeast-direct-from-factory': `<p>Many wholesale buyers prefer to purchase directly from a manufacturer rather than through several intermediaries. If you are considering buying yeast from a factory or finding a yeast distributor, this guide explains the advantages, where to start, and what to ask the producer.</p><h2>Why buy directly from the factory?</h2><p>Buying directly shortens the relationship between the customer and the producer. The main advantages are:</p><ul><li><strong>Clear information:</strong> you can ask directly about batch numbers, expiry dates, and storage conditions.</li><li><strong>Traceability:</strong> if there is a problem with the product, you know who is responsible for responding.</li><li><strong>Pack-size options:</strong> manufacturers usually offer a range of sizes for different uses.</li><li><strong>Fewer intermediaries:</strong> a shorter supply chain can make pricing clearer, although the final terms still depend on the agreement.</li></ul><h2>What should you know about a yeast manufacturer?</h2><p>A yeast factory is a facility that cultivates yeast, separates it, dries it, and packages the final product. Khuzestan Yeast Co. has operated in Dezful since 2002 and produces instant dry yeast using European technology, including technology associated with Frings of Austria, together with the expertise of Iranian specialists. The company has ISO 9001, ISO 22000, HACCP, ISO 14001, and ISO 45001 management systems and is a member of VH Berlin. In this context, a yeast factory means industrial-scale production under quality control.</p><h2>Which pack size should you buy from a factory?</h2><p>Manufacturers usually offer several sizes. The Khuzestan Yeast catalogue lists 70–100 g packs for household users, 450 g and 500 g packs for medium-sized needs, and 5 kg and 10 kg packs for professional use. Availability varies by brand and market. For more information on choosing a size, see the <a href="/en/products">yeast packaging options</a>.</p><figure><img alt="Which pack size should you buy from a factory?" src="/assets/blog/buy-yeast-direct-from-factory/image-02.webp"></figure><h2>Who can benefit from direct sales?</h2><p>Direct yeast sales can suit bakeries, industrial bread factories, pizzerias, manufacturers of flour-based products, and distributors. If you are interested in distribution, ask about working with an authorised <a href="/en/contact">yeast distributor</a>. Exact terms, such as minimum order quantity and payment arrangements, are set in consultation with the sales team.</p><h2>What should you ask the manufacturer?</h2><p>Before placing your first order, clarify the following:</p><ul><li>What type of yeast and technical product specification will you receive?</li><li>How long does each pack last, and how should it be stored?</li><li>How will the order be shipped, how will it be protected, and when will it arrive?</li><li>Can you get a sample for a bakery trial?</li><li>Which quality documents and certificates are available?</li></ul><p>When asking for a yeast price, request it alongside the pack specification and order volume so you can make a fair comparison. For more on the factors that affect cost, see our information on <a href="/en/contact">yeast pricing</a>.</p><figure><img alt="Questions to ask the manufacturer" src="/assets/blog/buy-yeast-direct-from-factory/image-03.webp"></figure><h2>Test before making a wholesale purchase</h2><p>Even when buying from a trusted manufacturer, a small bakery trial is worthwhile. Test the yeast in your own formula and real working conditions, and observe it over several batches. Once you are satisfied, increase the order. For a dependable supply, instant dry yeast is a common wholesale option. If you have more questions about choosing yeast, read our <a href="/en/blog/yeast-buying-guide">complete yeast buying guide</a> and article about <a href="/en/blog/yeast-types-for-baking">baker’s yeast</a>.</p><p>Overall, local yeast manufacturers and production in Iran show that buyers value domestic products they can trace. Direct purchasing is also possible by coordinating with the manufacturer’s sales team.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can I buy a small quantity directly from the factory?</h3><p>Order terms depend on the sales programme and coordination with the sales team. <a href="/en/contact">Contact us</a> to ask about available order sizes.</p><h3>Does the factory sell directly to bakeries?</h3><p>Depending on the area and current arrangements, sales may be direct or through distributors. You can ask through the <a href="/en/contact">website contact form</a>.</p>`,

  'yeast-buying-guide': `<h2>What is yeast, and why does choosing it matter?</h2><p>Yeast is one of the most important ingredients in bread and other dough products. This living organism ferments the sugars in dough and produces carbon dioxide, which makes the dough rise and gives bread a soft texture. Weak or old yeast can slow proofing, make bread heavy, and increase production waste.</p><p>Whether you run a bakery, a pastry workshop, or a wholesale shop, check the yeast type, quality, packaging, and supplier before you buy.</p><p>This yeast buying guide answers common questions: how to buy yeast, where to buy it, and what affects its price.</p><h2>Types of yeast</h2><p>To choose well, first learn the main types available on the market:</p><p>1. Fresh (compressed) yeast: high in moisture, it must be refrigerated and has a short shelf life. It is often used by businesses with daily access to a supplier.</p><p>Read more about fresh yeast in our article on <a href="/en/blog/fresh-vs-dry-yeast">fresh versus dry yeast</a>.</p><p>2. Active dry yeast: supplied as granules and should be activated in lukewarm water before use.</p><p>3. Instant yeast: has finer granules and can be mixed directly with flour without a separate activation step. Its convenience and long shelf life make it a common choice for bakeries, production facilities, and home baking.</p><p>Dry yeast, whether active or instant, lasts much longer than fresh yeast. Depending on the product and storage conditions, vacuum-packed dry yeast can keep for up to 24 months. This makes it easier to transport, store, and distribute—an important advantage for wholesale buyers. See the different formats and pack sizes on our <a href="/en/products">products page</a>.</p><h2>Where can you buy yeast?</h2><p>The main ways to source yeast are:</p><ul><li>Retail shops and supermarkets: suitable for home use and small packs.</li><li>Wholesalers and distributors: suitable for bakeries and businesses with medium consumption.</li><li>Authorised distributors: a reliable local distributor can help provide a steady supply.</li><li>Direct from the factory: suitable for wholesale buyers, distributors, and food manufacturers. Buying from the factory removes intermediaries and may allow more flexibility on price, volume, and delivery schedules.</li></ul><p>If you use yeast regularly every month, buying directly from a producer may offer both cost advantages and greater confidence in freshness and storage conditions.</p><p>See our page for <a href="/en/products">wholesale yeast products</a> for more information.</p><h2>How to identify good-quality yeast</h2><p>When buying yeast, check the following points.</p><p>For a more detailed guide, read <a href="/en/blog/how-to-identify-quality-yeast">how to identify good-quality yeast</a>.</p><ol><li>Production and expiry dates: old yeast has lower fermentation activity. Both dates should be clearly printed on the pack.</li><li>Packaging condition: a vacuum pack should be tightly sealed and leak-free. Air inside or a swollen pack can indicate improper storage.</li><li>Appearance and smell: granules should be uniform and dry, without a strong, sour, or mouldy smell.</li><li>Activity test: mix a small amount of yeast with lukewarm water and a little sugar. If it foams and produces even, fine bubbles within 10–15 minutes, it is active.</li><li>Permits and standards: ask the supplier for quality certificates and documents. Reputable producers commonly use systems such as ISO 9001, ISO 22000, and HACCP.</li><li>Performance in practice: the best measure is how the yeast performs in dough. Consistent proofing time, suitable volume, and repeatable results between batches are signs of quality.</li></ol><h2>Yeast packaging and shelf life</h2><p>Packaging affects both shelf life and ease of use. Small packs of around 70–125 g suit household use and retail. Packs of 450 g and 500 g are useful for small workshops and bakeries. Packs of 5 kg and 10 kg are designed for professional and industrial use.</p><p>Shelf life is closely related to packaging. Vacuum packs usually last longer than large bags. When buying wholesale, match your usage plan to the package’s shelf life. After opening, keep yeast away from heat and moisture and, preferably, refrigerate it.</p><h2>What affects the price of yeast?</h2><p>Yeast prices are not fixed; they change with market conditions, raw materials, and transport costs. To compare prices fairly, consider:</p><ul><li>Product type: fresh, active dry, and instant yeast have different prices.</li><li>Pack size and packaging: the price per kilogram is often lower in larger packs.</li><li>Brand and quality: brands can differ in fermentation strength and performance stability.</li><li>Order volume: wholesale orders may have more favourable pricing.</li><li>Supplier: buying directly from a factory can reduce intermediary costs.</li><li>Transport costs and payment terms.</li></ul><p>A common mistake is to choose only the lowest price. Weak yeast can increase bread waste and reduce product quality, raising your actual costs. Evaluate price together with quality, shelf life, and supply consistency.</p><p>For a current quotation, <a href="/en/contact">contact the sales team</a>.</p><h2>What matters most in baker’s yeast?</h2><p>For baker’s yeast, the most important factors are consistent performance, predictable fermentation speed, and a reliable supply. A bakery should be able to achieve the same result every day with the same amount of yeast. That is why many bakeries build a long-term relationship with a dependable supplier instead of changing brands repeatedly. If you are asking which yeast is best, the answer depends on your use, dough conditions, and these criteria—not just the brand name.</p><figure><img alt="Yeast buying guide: types, prices, and wholesale tips" src="/assets/blog/yeast-buying-guide/image-03.webp"></figure><h2>Khuzestan Yeast: Iranian production to international standards</h2><p><a href="/en/about">Khuzestan Yeast Co.</a> began operating in Dezful in 2002 and now produces instant dry yeast. The company combines European production technology, including technology associated with Frings of Austria, with the expertise of Iranian specialists. It holds ISO 9001, ISO 22000, HACCP, ISO 14001, ISO 45001, and halal certificates. Products are offered under four brands—Nanmaye, Dezmaye, Shetab, and XPOWER—in pack sizes from 70 g to 10 kg for traditional and commercial bakeries and food manufacturers.</p><figure data-rich-table=""><table><caption>Khuzestan Yeast: Iranian production to international standards</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col"><strong>Pack size</strong></th><th scope="col"><strong>Packaging type</strong></th><th scope="col"><strong>Shelf life</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>80 g</td><td>Sachet</td><td>12 months</td></tr><tr><td>70 g</td><td>Vacuum pack</td><td>24 months</td></tr><tr><td>90 g</td><td>Vacuum pack</td><td>24 months</td></tr><tr><td>100 g</td><td>Vacuum pack</td><td>24 months</td></tr><tr><td>400 g</td><td>Vacuum pack</td><td>24 months</td></tr><tr><td>450 g</td><td>Vacuum pack</td><td>24 months</td></tr><tr><td>500 g</td><td>Vacuum pack</td><td>24 months</td></tr><tr><td>5 kg (5,000 g)</td><td>Vacuum pack</td><td>24 months</td></tr><tr><td>10 kg</td><td>Bag</td><td>6 months</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><p>If you plan to buy yeast wholesale and directly from the factory, contact our sales team to discuss packaging, order volume, and cooperation terms.</p><h2>Yeast buying checklist</h2><ul><li>Choose a yeast type that fits your use: fresh, dry, or instant.</li><li>Check the production date, expiry date, and packaging condition.</li><li>Ask the supplier for certificates and quality documents.</li><li>Compare price together with quality and shelf life, not on its own.</li><li>For a regular supply, consider buying directly from the factory or an authorised distributor.</li><li>After opening, store the yeast in the refrigerator.</li></ul><p>Following these steps can make buying yeast easier and help you achieve more consistent baking results.</p><figure><img alt="Buying yeast" src="/assets/blog/yeast-buying-guide/image-04.webp"></figure><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><p>Is dry yeast better than fresh yeast? Each has its own uses. Dry yeast lasts longer and is easier to transport, while fresh yeast needs refrigeration and quick use.</p><p>Does instant yeast need to be activated? Usually not; instant yeast can be mixed directly with flour. Check the instructions on the product pack.</p><p>What should I ask before buying wholesale? Ask about the price per kilogram, packaging type, production date and shelf life, certificates, minimum order, and delivery time.</p><h2>Summary</h2><p>Choosing yeast depends on more than brand or price. Product type, production and expiry dates, packaging integrity, quality certificates, and real performance in dough all affect the result. Instant yeast is a practical choice for bakeries and production facilities because it is easy to use and keeps well. For a regular supply, buying directly from a factory can remove intermediaries and allow better agreement on price, volume, and delivery. Do not choose only the cheapest option: weak yeast can raise your true costs through increased waste. Khuzestan Yeast combines European technology with ISO, HACCP, and halal certificates, offers four brands—Nanmaye, Dezmaye, Shetab, and XPOWER—and supplies packs from 70 g to 10 kg. Contact the sales team for current prices and wholesale orders.</p>`,
};
